 "dotnet_build_calls": 0, "edit_calls": 2, "ended_on_rejection": false, "error": null, "exec_mode": "local", "failure_class": "solved", "failure_reason": null, "faults_served": 0, "final_answer_complete": true, "final_answer_present": true, "final_answer_source": "json", "final_workspace": "/workspace/openbench-ab/results/omp-cap30-b2/cap30-44/omp-44/cells/without/trig-omp-multistep/24/workspace", "fresh_subagents": 0, "harness": "omp", "harness_version": "omp/18.3.0 (/workspace/pi-check/cache5/bin/omp/7dd20b952257a3355c728716113cff772f7c5185/omp)", "harness_version_source": "host", "host": "grok-bot-vm-412615941", "image_digest": null, "last_activity_age_s": null, "list_has_generated": null, "main_agent_searched": false, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "model_context_window": 1000000, "model_max_tokens": 128000, "output_tail": "Root cause: `to_cents` truncates float imprecision (`19.99 * 100 == 1998.9999\u2026` \u2192 `1998`).\nI fixed the bug in `to_cents` in `ledger/currency.py`. It used `int(amount * 100)`, which drops the fractional part. Floats can't store most prices exactly, so `19.99 * 100` comes out as `1998.9999\u2026` and became 1998 cents instead of 1999. `0.29` and `4.35` hit the same problem, which is why the subtotal, quantity and discount tests failed.\n\nThe fix is `int(round(amount * 100))`, which rounds to the nearest cent instead of cutting it off.\n\npytest isn't installed here, so I called each test function in `tests/test_totals.py` and `tests/test_report.py` directly from Python. All five passed.\n\nOne limit: Python's `round` sends exact halves to the nearest even number. That doesn't matter for normal two-decimal prices. If inputs could have more than two decimals, `Decimal` with `ROUND_HALF_UP` would be the safer choice.", "paced_wait_s": 0.0, "permission_rejections": 0, "read_calls": 0, "replies_ok": 6, "replies_throttled": 0, "requests_cache_read": 71034, "requests_cache_write": 4991, "requests_cost_usd": 0.06335779999999999, "requests_coun